WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.831 --> 00:00:20.557
Swallow, little Swallow," said the Prince, "will you not stay with me one night longer?"

2
00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:27.263
"It is winter," answered the Swallow, "and the chill snow will soon be here.

3
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:34.264
In Egypt, the sun is warm on the green palm trees, and the crocodiles lie in the mud

4
00:00:34.584 --> 00:00:36.125
and look lazily about them.

5
00:00:36.965 --> 00:00:43.169
My companions are building a nest in the Temple of Baalbec, and the pink and white doves are

6
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:45.571
watching them and cooing to each other.

7
00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:53.997
Dear Prince, I must leave you, but I will never forget you, and next spring, I will bring

8
00:00:54.037 --> 00:00:58.880
you back two beautiful jewels in place of those you have given away.

9
00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:05.695
The ruby shall be redder than a red rose, and the sapphire shall be as blue as the

10
00:01:05.755 --> 00:01:06.516
great sea."

11
00:01:06.536 --> 00:01:14.880
"In the square below," said the Happy Prince, "there stands a little match girl.

12
00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:20.804
She has let her matches fall in the gutter, and they are all spoiled.

13
00:01:21.844 --> 00:01:28.168
Her father will beat her if she does not bring home some money, and she is crying.

14
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.101
She has no shoes or stockings, and her little head is bare.

15
00:01:34.902 --> 00:01:40.603
Pluck out my other eye and give it to her, and her father will not beat her."

16
00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:48.265
"I will stay with you one night longer," said the Swallow, "but I cannot pluck out your eye.

17
00:01:48.766 --> 00:01:50.606
You would be quite blind then."

18
00:01:50.626 --> 00:01:59.228
"Swallow, Swallow, little Swallow," said the Prince, "do as I command you."

19
00:02:00.089 --> 00:02:04.210
So he plucked out the Prince's other eye and darted down with it.

20
00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:09.811
He swooped past the match girl and slipped the jewel into the palm of her hand.

21
00:02:10.532 --> 00:02:16.893
"What a lovely bit of glass," cried the little girl, and she ran home, laughing.

22
00:02:17.753 --> 00:02:20.434
Then, the Swallow came back to the Prince.

23
00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:26.776
"You are blind now," he said, "so I will stay with you always."

24
00:02:27.836 --> 00:02:35.222
"No, little Swallow," said the poor Prince, "you must go away to Egypt."

25
00:02:36.242 --> 00:02:42.267
"I will stay with you always," said the Swallow, and he slept at the Prince's feet.

26
00:02:43.348 --> 00:02:49.172
All the next day, he sat on the Prince's shoulder and told him stories of what he had

27
00:02:49.292 --> 00:02:50.873
seen in strange lands.

28
00:02:51.774 --> 00:02:57.738
He told him of the red ibises, who stand in long rows on the banks of the Nile,

29
00:02:58.058 --> 00:03:05.423
and catch goldfish in their beaks; of the Sphinx, who is as old as the world itself and

30
00:03:05.563 --> 00:03:13.489
lives in the desert, and knows everything; of the merchants, who walk slowly by the side of their

31
00:03:13.589 --> 00:03:20.753
camels and carry amber beads in their hands; f King the of the Mountains of the Moon, who

32
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:29.037
is as black as ebony and worships a large crystal; and of the great green snake that sleeps

33
00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:33.838
in a palm tree and has twenty priests to feed it with honey cakes.

34
00:03:33.858 --> 00:03:46.622
"Dear little Swallow," said the Prince, "you tell me of marvelous things, but more marvelous than anything is

35
00:03:46.702 --> 00:03:49.583
the suffering of men and of women.

36
00:03:50.743 --> 00:03:55.245
There is no Mystery so great as Misery.

37
00:03:56.366 --> 00:04:02.009
Fly over my city, little Swallow, and tell me what you see there."

38
00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:10.495
So the Swallow flew over the great city and saw the rich making merry in their beautiful houses,

39
00:04:10.995 --> 00:04:13.517
while the beggars were sitting at the gates.

40
00:04:14.418 --> 00:04:22.823
He flew into dark lanes and saw the white faces of starving children looking out listlessly at the

41
00:04:22.883 --> 00:04:23.664
black streets.

42
00:04:24.848 --> 00:04:31.692
Under the archway of a bridge, two little boys were lying in one another's arms to try and

43
00:04:31.832 --> 00:04:33.233
keep themselves warm.

44
00:04:33.253 --> 00:04:36.034
"How hungry we are!"

45
00:04:36.054 --> 00:04:37.395
they said.

46
00:04:37.415 --> 00:04:44.079
"You must not lie here," shouted the Watchman, and they wandered out into the rain.

47
00:04:45.059 --> 00:04:48.881
Then, he flew back and told the Prince what he had seen.

48
00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:59.135
"I am covered with fine gold," said the Prince, "you must take it off, leaf by leaf, and

49
00:04:59.396 --> 00:05:05.419
give it to my poor; the living always think that gold can make them happy."

50
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:13.424
Leaf after leaf of the fine gold the Swallow picked off, till the Happy Prince looked quite dull

51
00:05:13.644 --> 00:05:14.205
and gray.

52
00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:22.574
Leaf after leaf of the fine gold he brought to the poor, and the children's faces grew rosier,

53
00:05:22.974 --> 00:05:26.156
and they laughed and played games in the street.

54
00:05:26.176 --> 00:05:28.557
"We have bread now!"

55
00:05:29.498 --> 00:05:30.158
they cried.

56
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:35.061
Then the snow came, and after the snow came the frost.

57
00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:43.025
The streets looked as if they were made of silver, they were so bright and glistening; long icicles

58
00:05:43.165 --> 00:05:51.822
like crystal daggers hung down from the eaves of the houses, everybody went about in furs, and the

59
00:05:51.862 --> 00:05:55.563
little boys wore scarlet caps and skated on the ice.

60
00:05:57.004 --> 00:06:05.047
The poor little Swallow grew colder and colder, but he would not leave the Prince; he loved him

61
00:06:05.207 --> 00:06:05.787
too well.

62
00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:12.650
He picked up crumbs outside the baker's door when the baker was not looking and tried to keep

63
00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.171
himself warm by flapping his wings.

64
00:06:15.932 --> 00:06:19.185
But at last, he knew that he was going to die.

65
00:06:19.885 --> 00:06:24.246
He had just strength to fly up to the Prince's shoulder once more.

66
00:06:24.266 --> 00:06:26.246
"Good-bye, dear Prince!"

67
00:06:26.987 --> 00:06:27.527
he murmured.

68
00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:29.887
"Will you let me kiss your hand?"

69
00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:37.990
"I am glad that you are going to Egypt at last, little Swallow," said the Prince.

70
00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:46.752
"You have stayed too long here; but you must kiss me on the lips, for I love you."

71
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.342
"It is not to Egypt that I am going," said the Swallow.

72
00:06:51.362 --> 00:06:54.123
"I am going to the House of Death.

73
00:06:54.683 --> 00:06:57.544
Death is the brother of Sleep, is he not?"

74
00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:03.827
And he kissed the Happy Prince on the lips and fell down dead at his feet.

75
00:07:04.727 --> 00:07:12.150
At that moment, a curious crack sounded inside the statue, as if something had broken.

76
00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:17.776
The fact is that the lead heart had snapped right in two.

77
00:07:17.796 --> 00:07:20.839
It certainly was a dreadfully hard frost.

78
00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:28.105
Early the next morning, the Mayor was walking in the square below in company with the Town Councilors.

79
00:07:28.865 --> 00:07:33.489
As they passed the column, he looked up at the statue: "Dear me!

80
00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.411
How shabby the Happy Prince looks!"

81
00:07:37.292 --> 00:07:37.712
he said.

82
00:07:37.732 --> 00:07:40.134
"How shabby indeed!"

83
00:07:41.195 --> 00:07:46.880
cried the Town Councilors, who always agreed with the Mayor, and they went up to look at it.

84
00:07:47.872 --> 00:07:53.814
"The ruby has fallen out of his sword, his eyes are gone, and he is golden no longer,"

85
00:07:54.735 --> 00:07:55.355
said the Mayor.

86
00:07:55.375 --> 00:07:59.257
"In fact, he is little better than a beggar!"

87
00:07:59.277 --> 00:08:03.638
"Little better than a beggar," said the Town Councilors.

88
00:08:03.658 --> 00:08:08.000
"And here is actually a dead bird at his feet!"

89
00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:09.601
continued the Mayor.

90
00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:15.563
"We must really issue a proclamation that birds are not to be allowed to die here."

91
00:08:16.284 --> 00:08:18.545
And the Town Clerk made a note of the suggestion.

92
00:08:19.366 --> 00:08:23.189
So they pulled down the statue of the Happy Prince.

93
00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:31.015
"As he is no longer beautiful, he is no longer useful," said the Art Professor at the University.

94
00:08:31.856 --> 00:08:38.421
Then, they melted the statue in a furnace, and the Mayor held a meeting of the Corporation to

95
00:08:38.461 --> 00:08:40.523
decide what was to be done with the metal.

96
00:08:40.543 --> 00:08:47.780
"We must have another statue, of course," he said, "and it shall be a statue of myself."

97
00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:53.804
"Of myself," said each of the Town Councilors, and they quarreled.

98
00:08:54.665 --> 00:08:56.346
They quarreled for days.

99
00:08:56.366 --> 00:08:58.807
"What a strange thing!"

100
00:08:59.788 --> 00:09:02.429
said the overseer of the workmen at the foundry.

101
00:09:02.449 --> 00:09:06.311
"This broken lead heart will not melt in the furnace.

102
00:09:06.992 --> 00:09:08.413
We must throw it away."

103
00:09:09.513 --> 00:09:13.516
So they threw it on a dust heap where the dead Swallow was also lying.

104
00:09:14.556 --> 00:09:21.459
"Bring me the two most precious things in the city," said God to one of His Angels, and

105
00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:25.000
the Angel brought Him the lead heart and the dead bird.

106
00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:35.385
"You have rightly chosen," said God, "for in my garden of Paradise, this little bird shall sing for

107
00:09:35.465 --> 00:09:41.707
evermore, and in my city of gold, the Happy Prince shall praise me."
